use std
into esch.amni
dialect js

This file is a library built for the web as a wasm module; its The exported function `es_dups()` empties `AD` with `clear` and, for every part a in `AQ` with a number of at least 1, when a part b with a lower index has the same prefix and the same number, appends b and a to `AD` (only the first such b). It calls `es_views` and returns the length of `AD` over 2.

`main()` does nothing. JavaScript drives it through its exported functions (every name starting with es_ is exported). It is the schematic engine of the Electronics workspace: it draws the part symbols, turns wires, pins and labels into nets, places junction dots, checks the electrical rules, finds what is under the pointer, drags wires along with a moved part and numbers the parts. Every length is in millimeters, x to the right and y up. The drawing grid is the constant `GD` equal to 2.54 and two points touch when they are within the constant `ETOL` equal to 0.001. Pin electrical types: 0 passive, 1 input, 2 output, 3 bidirectional, 4 tri-state, 5 power input, 6 power output, 7 open collector, 8 not connected, 9 unspecified.

```amni
extern fn es_view_f64(id i32, xs real[:]) = "es_view_f64";
extern fn es_view_i32(id i32, xs i32[:]) = "es_view_i32";
```

Module state. Ints, all starting at 0: `NQ` (parts), `NW` (wires), `NL` (labels), `NX` (no-connect flags), `NK` (pins), `NE` (nets), `NA` (parts to number), `NF` (reference prefixes). Growable f64 arrays: `SY` (5 per symbol pin), `SR` (7 per symbol drawing primitive), `QP` (10 per part), `QT` (pin type overrides), `WR` (5 per wire), `LB` (5 per label), `XF` (3 per no-connect flag), `PN` (10 per pin), `QB` (4 per part), `NI` (8 per net), `ER` (6 per check result), `JC` (3 per junction), `AQ` (5 per part to number), `BB` (4 values). Growable i32 arrays: `UF` (1 per node), `RN` (1 per node), `NT` (1 per node), `TC` (10 per net), `AN` (1 per part to number), `AO` (1 per part to number), `US` (used numbers), `MW` (wire ends to move), `ML` (labels to move), `AD` (duplicate pairs), `GF` (1 per label: 1 for a ground label). More growable f64 arrays: `LO` (2 per label: text side and symbol rotation), `PB` (6 per part to place), `ST` (2 per pin: pin stub end).

A symbol pin in `SY` is x, y (where a wire connects), dx, dy (the unit direction pointing from the body out through the pin) and type. A drawing primitive in `SR` is an operation code and six values a, b, c, d, e, f: 1 a line from (a, b) to (c, d); 2 a rectangle outline with corners (a, b) and (c, d); 3 a circle with center (a, b) and radius c; 4 an arc with center (a, b), radius c, from angle d to angle e in radians counterclockwise; 5 a filled triangle (a, b), (c, d), (e, f). Unused values are 0.

A part in `QP` is kind, n (pin count), m (left pins), w (body width in grid steps), x, y, rot (quarter turns counterclockwise, 0 to 3), mir (1 mirrors left-right before rotating), sheet, and the index in `QT` of the type of its first pin or minus 1 to use the symbol's own types. A wire in `WR` is x0, y0, x1, y1, sheet. A label in `LB` is x, y, sheet, kind (0 local, 1 global, 2 power) and name id (equal ids mean equal names). A no-connect flag in `XF` is x, y, sheet. A pin in `PN` is x, y, sheet, part, symbol pin index (from 0), type, net, dx, dy and no-connect (1 when a flag sits on it). A part box in `QB` is its world left, bottom, right, top. A net record in `NI` is best label, best label kind (both minus 1 when none), first pin, pin count, wire count, label count, 0, 0. `TC` counts the pins of each type 0 to 9 on a net. A check result in `ER` is code, x, y, sheet, a, b. A junction in `JC` is x, y, sheet. A part to number in `AQ` is prefix id, number (0 when not yet numbered), sheet, x, y.

Nodes. The node index of pin p is p, of wire w is `NK` plus w, of label l is `NK` plus `NW` plus l, and of no-connect flag f is `NK` plus `NW` plus `NL` plus f.

The exported function `es_views()` registers every array JavaScript reads or writes: f64 views 1 `SY`, 2 `SR`, 3 `QP`, 4 `QT`, 5 `WR`, 6 `LB`, 7 `XF`, 8 `PN`, 9 `QB`, 10 `NI`, 11 `ER`, 12 `JC`, 13 `AQ`, 14 `BB`, 19 `LO`, 20 `PB`, 21 `ST` through es_view_f64, and i32 views 15 `NT`, 16 `AN`, 17 `TC`, 18 `AD`, 22 `GF` through es_view_i32.

Sizing helpers. `zf(xs, n, x)` takes an f64 growable array by reference: xs is emptied, then gets `reserve`(n), then is resized to n entries equal to x. `zi(xs, n, x)` takes an i32 growable array by reference and does the same, resizing to n entries equal to x as i32. Every array in this file is sized only through these two helpers or emptied with its own `clear`; no block defines other sizing helpers.

The exported function `es_setup(nq, nw, nl, nx, nt)` takes five i32 counts and stores the first four in `NQ`, `NW`, `NL` and `NX`; with `zf` it sizes `QP` to 10·nq zeros, `WR` to 5·nw zeros, `LB` to 5·nl zeros, `XF` to 3·nx zeros and `QT` to nt zeros, `GF` gets `zi` of nl zeros; `BB` gets `zf` of 4 zeros. It calls `es_views` and returns 0.

Symbol writers. `spin(x, y, dx, dy, t)` appends those five values to `SY`. `sop(o, a, b, c, d, e, f)` appends those seven values to `SR`. `sln(a, b, c, d)` calls `sop`(1, a, b, c, d, 0, 0). `pinl(x, y, dx, dy, t, len)` calls `spin`(x, y, dx, dy, t) and then `sln`(x, y, x minus len·dx, y minus len·dy), the pin's lead toward the body. `arrow(tx, ty, dx, dy)` draws a filled arrowhead with its tip at (tx, ty) pointing along (dx, dy): with (ux, uy) equal to (dx, dy) divided by its length and the base center (bx, by) equal to the tip minus 0.9·(ux, uy), it calls `sop`(5, tx, ty, bx minus 0.45·uy, by plus 0.45·ux, bx plus 0.45·uy, by minus 0.45·ux).

`dio()` draws the diode body shared by kinds 5 to 8, with g equal to `GD`: pin 1 (cathode) `pinl`(2g, 0, 1, 0, 0, 2g minus 1.27), pin 2 (anode) `pinl`(minus 2g, 0, minus 1, 0, 0, 2g minus 1.27), then lines from (minus 1.27, minus 1.27) to (minus 1.27, 1.27), from (minus 1.27, 1.27) to (1.27, 0), from (1.27, 0) to (minus 1.27, minus 1.27) and the bar from (1.27, minus 1.27) to (1.27, 1.27).

`bjt(pnp)` draws a bipolar transistor, with g equal to `GD`: pin 1 (base) `pinl`(minus 2g, 0, minus 1, 0, 0, 2g), pin 2 (collector) `pinl`(g, 2g, 0, 1, 0, 2g minus 2.3), pin 3 (emitter) `pinl`(g, minus 2g, 0, minus 1, 0, 2g minus 2.3); lines from (0, minus 1.9) to (0, 1.9), from (0, 0.8) to (g, 2.3) and from (0, minus 0.8) to (g, minus 2.3); a circle centered (1.0, 0) with radius 3.0; then when pnp is 0 `arrow`(2.032, minus 2.0, 2.54, minus 1.5), otherwise `arrow`(0.762, minus 1.25, minus 2.54, 1.5).

`fet(pch)` draws a MOSFET, with g equal to `GD`: pin 1 (gate) `pinl`(minus 2g, 0, minus 1, 0, 0, 2g minus 0.9), pin 2 (drain) `pinl`(g, 2g, 0, 1, 0, 2g minus 1.7), pin 3 (source) `pinl`(g, minus 2g, 0, minus 1, 0, 2g minus 1.7); lines from (minus 0.9, minus 1.9) to (minus 0.9, 1.9), from (0, 1.2) to (0, 2.2), from (0, minus 0.5) to (0, 0.5), from (0, minus 2.2) to (0, minus 1.2), from (0, 1.7) to (g, 1.7), from (0, minus 1.7) to (g, minus 1.7), from (0, 0) to (g, 0) and from (g, 0) to (g, minus 1.7); a circle centered (1.0, 0) with radius 3.0; then when pch is 0 `arrow`(0.1, 0, minus 1, 0), otherwise `arrow`(2.2, 0, 1, 0).

`xtal()` draws the crystal body: lines from (minus 1.6, minus 1.5) to (minus 1.6, 1.5) and from (1.6, minus 1.5) to (1.6, 1.5) and the rectangle with corners (minus 0.9, minus 2.0) and (0.9, 2.0).

`sym(kind, n, m, w)` takes four i32 values, empties `SY` and `SR` with `clear`, writes the pins and drawing of one symbol at the origin and returns the pin count (the length of `SY` over 5). g is `GD`. Kind 1, resistor: `pinl`(minus 2g, 0, minus 1, 0, 0, g), `pinl`(2g, 0, 1, 0, 0, g), then a zigzag of lines joining the seven points with x equal to minus g plus i·g/3 for i from 0 to 6 and y equal to 0 for i 0 and 6, 0.9 for odd i and minus 0.9 for i 2 and 4. Kind 2, capacitor: `pinl`(minus 2g, 0, minus 1, 0, 0, 2g minus 0.5), `pinl`(2g, 0, 1, 0, 0, 2g minus 0.5), lines from (minus 0.5, minus 1.6) to (minus 0.5, 1.6) and from (0.5, minus 1.6) to (0.5, 1.6). Kind 3, polarized capacitor with pin 1 positive: `pinl`(minus 2g, 0, minus 1, 0, 0, 2g minus 0.5), `pinl`(2g, 0, 1, 0, 0, 2g minus 0.75), the line from (minus 0.5, minus 1.6) to (minus 0.5, 1.6), the arc (operation 4) centered (3.2, 0) with radius 2.45 from angle π minus 0.6 to π plus 0.6, and the plus sign lines from (minus 2.0, 1.4) to (minus 1.2, 1.4) and from (minus 1.6, 1.0) to (minus 1.6, 1.8). Kind 4, inductor: `pinl`(minus 2g, 0, minus 1, 0, 0, g), `pinl`(2g, 0, 1, 0, 0, g) and for k from 0 to 3 the arc centered (minus g plus g/4 plus k·g/2, 0) with radius g/4 from angle 0 to π. Kind 5, diode: `dio`(). Kind 6, LED: `dio`(), lines from (0.2, 1.5) to (1.3, 2.6) and from (1.3, 1.5) to (2.4, 2.6), `arrow`(1.3, 2.6, 1, 1) and `arrow`(2.4, 2.6, 1, 1). Kind 7, Schottky diode: `dio`() and lines from (1.27, 1.27) to (1.87, 1.27), from (1.87, 1.27) to (1.87, 0.77), from (1.27, minus 1.27) to (0.67, minus 1.27) and from (0.67, minus 1.27) to (0.67, minus 0.77). Kind 8, Zener diode: `dio`() and lines from (1.27, 1.27) to (0.77, 1.77) and from (1.27, minus 1.27) to (1.77, minus 1.77). Kind 9, NPN transistor: `bjt`(0). Kind 10, PNP transistor: `bjt`(1). Kind 11, N-channel MOSFET: `fet`(0). Kind 12, P-channel MOSFET: `fet`(1). Kind 13, a box with n pins: hw is (the larger of w and 4)·g/2, r is the larger of m and n minus m and 1, and top is ((r minus 1) minus (r minus 1) div 2)·g; for i from 0 to m minus 1 `pinl`(minus hw minus g, top minus i·g, minus 1, 0, 3, g); for j from 0 to n minus m minus 1 `pinl`(hw plus g, top minus j·g, 1, 0, 3, g); then the rectangle with corners (minus hw, top plus g) and (hw, top minus r·g). Kind 14, single-row connector: with top equal to ((n minus 1) div 2)·g, for i from 0 to n minus 1 `pinl`(minus 2g, top minus i·g, minus 1, 0, 0, g), then the rectangle with corners (minus g, top plus g/2) and (g, top minus (n minus 1)·g minus g/2). Kind 15, two-pin crystal: `pinl`(minus 2g, 0, minus 1, 0, 0, 2g minus 1.6), `pinl`(2g, 0, 1, 0, 0, 2g minus 1.6), `xtal`(). Kind 16, four-pin crystal: `pinl`(minus 2g, 0, minus 1, 0, 0, 2g minus 1.6), `pinl`(0, minus 2g, 0, minus 1, 0, 2g minus 2.0), `pinl`(2g, 0, 1, 0, 0, 2g minus 1.6), `pinl`(0, 2g, 0, 1, 0, 2g minus 2.0), `xtal`(). Kind 17, push button: `pinl`(minus 2g, 0, minus 1, 0, 0, g), `pinl`(2g, 0, 1, 0, 0, g), circles centered (minus 2.09, 0) and (2.09, 0) with radius 0.45, lines from (minus 2.6, 1.3) to (2.6, 1.3), from (0, 1.3) to (0, 2.5) and from (minus 0.9, 2.5) to (0.9, 2.5). Kind 18, fuse: `pinl`(minus 2g, 0, minus 1, 0, 0, g), `pinl`(2g, 0, 1, 0, 0, g), the rectangle with corners (minus g, minus 0.9) and (g, 0.9) and the line from (minus g, 0) to (g, 0). Kind 19, ferrite bead: `pinl`(minus 2g, 0, minus 1, 0, 0, g), `pinl`(2g, 0, 1, 0, 0, g), the rectangle with corners (minus g, minus 1.0) and (g, 1.0) and the line from (minus 1.6, minus 1.6) to (1.6, 1.6). Kind 20, power rail: `spin`(0, 0, 0, minus 1, 6), lines from (0, 0) to (0, 1.27) and from (minus 1.27, 1.27) to (1.27, 1.27). Kind 21, ground: `spin`(0, 0, 0, 1, 6), lines from (0, 0) to (0, minus 1.27), from (minus 1.9, minus 1.27) to (1.9, minus 1.27), from (minus 1.2, minus 1.9) to (1.2, minus 1.9) and from (minus 0.5, minus 2.5) to (0.5, minus 2.5). Kind 22, mounting hole: no pins, circles centered (0, 0) with radius 2.0 and 1.2. Kind 23, double-row connector: with r equal to n div 2 and top equal to ((r minus 1) div 2)·g, for k from 0 to r minus 1 `pinl`(minus 2g, top minus k·g, minus 1, 0, 0, g) and then `pinl`(2g, top minus k·g, 1, 0, 0, g), then the rectangle with corners (minus g, top plus g/2) and (g, top minus (r minus 1)·g minus g/2). Any other kind writes nothing.

`bbox()` sets `BB` to the left, bottom, right and top of the current symbol: the smallest and largest x and y over every pin point in `SY` and over every primitive in `SR` (for lines and rectangles both points, for circles and arcs the center plus and minus the radius in x and y, for triangles all three points). When `SY` and `SR` are both empty `BB` is minus g, minus g, g, g with g equal to `GD`.

The exported function `es_sym(kind, n, m, w)` takes four i32 values, calls `sym` with them, then `bbox`, then `es_views`, and returns the pin count.

World transform. `rx(q, px, py)` and `ry(q, px, py)` return the world x and world y of the local point (px, py) of part q (record at 10·q in `QP`): x1 is minus px when mir is 1 and px otherwise, y1 is py; with r equal to rot as an int modulo 4 (made non-negative), the rotated point is (x1, y1) for r 0, (minus y1, x1) for r 1, (minus x1, minus y1) for r 2 and (y1, minus x1) for r 3; the result adds the part's x (for `rx`) or y (for `ry`) and is rounded to 4 decimals like JavaScript's Math.round(v·10000)/10000. `rdx(q, ux, uy)` and `rdy(q, ux, uy)` return the rotated direction the same way without adding the part position and without rounding.

Geometry. `samept(ax, ay, bx, by)` is 1 when the absolute differences of x and of y are both at most `ETOL`, otherwise 0. `onseg(px, py, ax, ay, bx, by)` is 1 when the distance from point P to the segment AB is at most `ETOL`, otherwise 0: with d equal to B minus A and L2 its squared length, t is 0 when L2 is below 1e-18 and otherwise ((P minus A) dot d) over L2 clamped to 0 through 1, and the distance is the length of P minus (A plus t·d). `inner(px, py, ax, ay, bx, by)` is 1 when `onseg` is 1 and P is `samept` with neither A nor B, otherwise 0.

Union-find. `ufind(i)` follows `UF` from i to the root (an entry equal to its own index) and then sets `UF` of every node on that walk to the root, returning the root. `uunion(i, j)` finds both roots and, when they differ, sets `UF` of the larger root to the smaller root.

`pins()` builds the world pins and part boxes: `PN` is emptied with `clear` and `QB` gets `zf` of 4·`NQ` zeros. For every part q: it calls `sym` with the part's kind, n, m and w as i32 and then `bbox`; for every symbol pin k (record at 5·k in `SY`) with local point (px, py), direction (ux, uy) and type t, where t is replaced by `QT` at the part's type index plus k when that index is at least 0 and that position is below the length of `QT`, it appends `rx`(q, px, py), `ry`(q, px, py), the part's sheet, q, k, t, minus 1, `rdx`(q, ux, uy), `rdy`(q, ux, uy) and 0 to `PN`. The part box is the smallest and largest world x and y over the four corners of `BB` transformed by `rx` and `ry`. `NK` becomes the length of `PN` over 10.

`jadd(x, y, s)` appends x, y, s to `JC` unless a junction already in `JC` on sheet s is `samept` with (x, y).

`jcount(x, y, s)` counts the connections at a point on sheet s: 1 for every wire on sheet s with an end `samept` with (x, y), plus 2 for every wire on sheet s where `inner`(x, y, its ends) is 1, plus 1 for every pin on sheet s `samept` with (x, y).

`erc(code, x, y, s, a, b)` appends those six values to `ER` when the length of `ER` is below 6 times 8192.

`touched(x, y, s, w)` is 1 when the point (x, y) on sheet s touches something other than wire w: a wire v other than w on sheet s with `onseg` of the point and v's ends, or a pin, a label or a no-connect flag on sheet s `samept` with the point; otherwise 0.

The exported function `es_net()` returns an i32. It calls `pins`. With nn equal to `NK` plus `NW` plus `NL` plus `NX`, `UF` gets `zi` of nn entries each equal to its own index, and `RN` and `NT` get `zi` of nn entries minus 1. A wire's sheet is its fifth value, a label's its third and a flag's its third. Connections, only between items on the same sheet: every pair of wires a below b is joined with `uunion` when either end of b is `onseg` of wire a or either end of a is `onseg` of wire b; every pin is joined with every wire it is `onseg` of; every pair of pins a below b whose points are `samept` is joined; every label is joined with every wire its point is `onseg` of and with every pin whose point is `samept` with it. Every pair of labels a below b with equal name ids is joined when both kinds are at least 1 (on any sheets) or when both kinds are 0 and the sheets are equal. For every no-connect flag and every pin on the same sheet with `samept` points the pin's no-connect value becomes 1. Nets: `NE` becomes 0 and for every pin p in order with r equal to `ufind`(p), when `RN` of r is minus 1 it becomes `NE` and `NE` grows by 1; then every node i gets `NT` of i equal to `RN` of `ufind`(i), and every pin's net value in `PN` becomes `NT` of the pin. `NI` gets `zf` of 8·`NE` zeros and then every net gets minus 1 for best label, best kind and first pin; `TC` gets `zi` of 10·`NE` zeros. For every pin p with net k: the first pin of k becomes p when it is minus 1, its pin count grows by 1 and `TC` at 10·k plus the pin type as an int grows by 1. For every wire with net k at least 0 the wire count of k grows by 1. For every label l with net k at least 0 the label count grows by 1, and when the best kind of k is minus 1 or below the label's kind, the best label becomes l and the best kind the label's kind. Junctions: `JC` is emptied with `clear`; for every end of every wire and for every pin, when `jcount` at its point and sheet is at least 3 it calls `jadd` there. Then it calls `checks` and `es_views` and returns `NE`.

`checks()` empties `ER` with `clear` and appends the check results. For a pin p, its location is its x, y and sheet, its net k and the net's record in `NI` and `TC`. Code 1, unconnected pin: the pin count of k is 1, the pin type is not 8, its no-connect value is 0 and the best kind of k is not 2; `erc`(1, location, p, minus 1). Code 2, no-connect flag on a connected pin: its no-connect value is 1 and the pin count of k is at least 2 or the wire count or label count of k is at least 1; `erc`(2, location, p, minus 1). Code 9, a not-connected pin is wired: the type is 8 and the pin count of k is at least 2; `erc`(9, location, p, minus 1). Then for every net k: code 3, two outputs on one net: when the count of pins of type 2 or 6 on k is at least 2, with a the first and b the second such pin in pin order, `erc`(3, the location of b, a, b); code 4, power input not driven: when k has a pin of type 5, no pin of type 6 and its best kind is not 2, `erc`(4, the location of its first type-5 pin, that pin, minus 1); code 5, input not driven: when k has a pin of type 1, its pin count is at least 2, it has no pin of types 0, 2, 3, 4, 6, 7 or 9 and its best kind is not 2, `erc`(5, the location of its first type-1 pin, that pin, minus 1). Code 6, dangling wire end: for every wire w and each end e (0 for x0, y0 and 1 for x1, y1) where `touched`(the end, the wire's sheet, w) is 0, `erc`(6, the end, the sheet, w, e). Code 7, label touching nothing: for every label l where no wire on its sheet has the label point `onseg` and no pin on its sheet is `samept` with it, `erc`(7, the label point and sheet, l, minus 1). Code 8, label used once: for every label l with kind below 2 where no other label has the same name id with, for kind 1, a kind of at least 1 on any sheet, or for kind 0, kind 0 on the same sheet, `erc`(8, the label point and sheet, l, minus 1).

The exported function `es_hit(x, y, s, tol)` takes reals x, y, tol and an i32 sheet s and returns an i32: for the first label on sheet s whose point is within tol of (x, y) in both x and y, `NQ` plus `NW` plus its index; otherwise for the wire on sheet s with the smallest distance to (x, y) when that distance is at most tol, `NQ` plus its index; otherwise among the parts on sheet s whose box in `QB` grown by tol on every side contains (x, y), the index of the one with the smallest box area; otherwise minus 1. It reads `QB`, so `es_net` must have run.

The exported function `es_drag(q, ddx, ddy)` takes an i32 part q and reals ddx and ddy and moves part q with its connections. `MW` and `ML` are emptied with `clear`. For every pin of q in `PN` (its part value equals q), every wire end on the pin's sheet `samept` with the pin point is marked by appending 2·wire plus end to `MW` unless already present, and every label on that sheet whose point is `samept` with the pin point is appended to `ML` unless already present. Then every marked wire end and label point moves by (ddx, ddy) rounded to 4 decimals like JavaScript's Math.round(v·10000)/10000, the part's x and y in `QP` move the same way, it calls `pins` and `es_views` and returns the length of `MW`.

The exported function `es_aset(n, nf)` takes two i32 counts, stores them in `NA` and `NF`, sizes `AQ` with `zf` to 5·n zeros and `AN` and `AO` with `zi` to n zeros, calls `es_views` and returns 0.

`before(a, b)` is 1 when part a (record at 5·a in `AQ`) comes before part b in numbering order, otherwise 0. With band of a part equal to the floor of (minus its y) over (4·`GD`): compare the sheets first (smaller first), then the bands (smaller first), then x (smaller first), then the indices (smaller first).

`nextfree(p)` returns the smallest number c from 1 upward where `US` at p·(`NA` plus 2) plus c is 0, sets that entry to 1 and returns c.

The exported function `es_annot(mode)` takes an i32 mode and numbers the parts in `AQ` into `AN`. `AO` is set to 0 through `NA` minus 1 and insertion-sorted with `before`. `US` gets `zi` of `NF`·(`NA` plus 2) zeros. Mode 1 renumbers everything: walking `AO` in order every part gets `nextfree` of its prefix. Mode 0 keeps good numbers: walking `AO`, a part with number c at least 1 keeps it unless an earlier part in that walk with the same prefix already kept c, in which case it gets 0; a kept c at most `NA` plus 1 marks `US` at prefix·(`NA` plus 2) plus c with 1; a part with number 0 gets 0. Then walking `AO` again every part whose `AN` is 0 gets `nextfree` of its prefix. It calls `es_views` and returns how many parts got a number different from their number in `AQ`.

`main()` does nothing.
The exported function `es_lor()` orients every label for drawing, using the pins in `PN` from the last `es_net` or `es_drag`. It sizes `LO` with `zf` to 2·`NL` zeros and calls `es_views`. For every label i below `NL` with x equal to LB[5i], y equal to LB[5i plus 1], sheet s equal to LB[5i plus 2] and kind equal to LB[5i plus 3]: the side d is found from the first pin k below `NK` with PN[10k plus 2] equal to s and `samept`(PN[10k], PN[10k plus 1], x, y): d is minus 1.0 when PN[10k plus 7] is below minus 0.5 and 1.0 otherwise. When no pin matches, the first wire w below `NW` with WR[5w plus 4] equal to s whose start (WR[5w], WR[5w plus 1]) or end (WR[5w plus 2], WR[5w plus 3]) is `samept` with (x, y) decides: its other x is WR[5w plus 2] when the start matched and WR[5w] otherwise, and d is minus 1.0 when that other x is greater than x plus 1e-3, otherwise 1.0. With no pin and no wire, d is 1.0. When kind is 2 (a power symbol), with g equal to GF[i] and b equal to minus 1.0 when g is 1 and 1.0 otherwise, the symbol is sideways when some other label m (m not i) has LB[5m plus 2] equal to s, the absolute value of LB[5m] minus x below 1e-3, (LB[5m plus 1] minus y) times b greater than 0.0 and the absolute value of LB[5m plus 1] minus y at most 3.811; a sideways power symbol gets LO[2i] equal to d and LO[2i plus 1] equal to 3.0 when g is 1 and d is negative, 1.0 when g is 1 and d is not negative, 1.0 when g is 0 and d is negative and 3.0 otherwise; an upright one gets 0.0 and 0.0. Every other label gets LO[2i] equal to d and LO[2i plus 1] equal to 0.0. It returns `NL`.
The exported function `es_pset(n)` takes an i32 count, sizes `PB` with `zf` to 6·n zeros, calls `es_views` and returns 0. JavaScript then writes each part's symbol box (min x, min y, max x, max y) into PB[6i] to PB[6i plus 3].
`r4(v)` rounds a real to 4 decimals like JavaScript's Math.round(v·10000)/10000. `gsnap(v)` is `r4` of JavaScript's Math.round(v/2.54) times 2.54.
The exported function `es_pack(n)` lays out n parts from `PB` in rows on the 2.54 grid, left to right and then downward. For part i its box is b0, b1, b2, b3 equal to PB[6i] to PB[6i plus 3], its width w is b2 minus b0 plus 40.64 and its height is b3 minus b1 plus 17.78. The row limit W is the larger of 101.6 and the square root of 3 times the sum over all parts of width times height. Starting with x, y and the row height r all 0.0, for each part in order: when x is greater than 0.0 and x plus w is greater than W, x becomes 0.0, y becomes y minus r and r becomes 0.0; then PB[6i plus 4] becomes `gsnap`(x minus b0 plus 20.32) and PB[6i plus 5] becomes `gsnap`(y minus b3 minus 7.62); x grows by w and r becomes the larger of r and the part height. It returns n.
The exported function `es_row(n, x0, y0, step)` places n new board parts in a row from `PB`: for i below n, PB[6i plus 4] becomes `r4`(x0 plus step/2 plus step·i) and PB[6i plus 5] becomes `r4`(y0). It returns n.
The exported function `es_stubs()` gives every pin in `PN` a short wire end 2 grid steps out along its direction: it sizes `ST` with `zf` to 2·`NK` zeros, calls `es_views`, and for every pin k below `NK` sets ST[2k] to `r4`(PN[10k] plus 5.08·PN[10k plus 7]) and ST[2k plus 1] to `r4`(PN[10k plus 1] plus 5.08·PN[10k plus 8]). It returns `NK`.
